//? Title: Logistic Regression: Stable Likelihoods and Probabilities
//? Subtitle: Fit logits, then distinguish calibration from classification
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Binary observations need probabilities rather than unrestricted linear predictions. We fit a
small frequency table with an exact logistic optimum, inspect extreme-logit losses and check
the gradient at zero. A multinomial example connects the binary score to softmax without
claiming that a small training loss alone establishes calibration.
)
\toc

> The method

With logit z=xᵀw, probability p=sigmoid(z), the Bernoulli negative log likelihood is

$$
\ell(z,y)=\max(z,0)-yz+\log(1+e^{-|z|}),\qquad \partial_z\ell=\sigma(z)-y.
$$
\alttext(The stable binary loss avoids exponentiating a large positive logit; its score is predicted probability minus the observed binary label.)

The formula follows from -y log p-(1-y)log(1-p), rearranged to avoid overflow. The implementation
uses a derivative at zero consistent with sigmoid(z)-y, avoiding a spurious max/abs kink.
For grouped observations with common features, the optimum probability is the group's success
fraction whenever the logits can represent those fractions. Complete separation can send the
unpenalised optimum to infinite coefficients; finite iterations do not prove a finite MLE.
Classification thresholds encode a decision rule, while calibration compares probabilities
with event frequencies. A dense gradient step costs O(Np).

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/logistic_regression_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/logistic_regression_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/logistic_regression_concept.tikz > build/logistic_regression_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/logistic_regression_concept.svg build/logistic_regression_concept.dvi > build/logistic_regression_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/logistic_regression_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
