> Simple examples

At x=-1 one of three labels is positive; at x=1 two of three are positive. Intercept zero and slope log(2) give the exact fitted probabilities 1/3 and 2/3.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/logistic_regression_groups.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto dtype = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto x = n::tensor({-1, -1, -1, 1, 1, 1}, {6, 1}, dtype),
             X = n::concat({n::ones_like(x), x}, 1),
             y = n::tensor({0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1}, {6, 1}, dtype);
  auto w = n::zeros({2, 1}, dtype).requires_grad_();
  n::OptimizerConfig config;
  config.kind = n::OptimizerKind::Adam;
  n::Optimizer optimizer({w}, .05, config);
  for (int i = 0; i < 500; ++i)
    optimizer.minimize(n::sigmoid_cross_entropy(n::matmul(X, w), y));
  const auto q = n::linspace(-2, 2, 81, dtype).reshape({81, 1}),
             p = n::sigmoid(n::matmul(n::concat({n::ones_like(q), q}, 1), w));
  if (std::abs(w.at({0, 0})) > 1e-7 || std::abs(w.at({1, 0}) - std::log(2.)) > 1e-7)
    return 1;
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(q.to_vector<double>(), p.to_vector<double>(), "fitted probability")
      .points({-1, 1}, {1. / 3, 2. / 3}, "group rates")
      .title("Logistic probabilities match observed group rates")
      .x_label("x")
      .y_label("P(y=1)")
      .y_limits(0, 1.1);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.size(850, 420).save_svg("build/logistic_regression_groups.svg");
  std::print("Intercept {:.9f}; slope {:.9f}; expected slope {:.9f}.\n", w.at({0, 0}),
             w.at({1, 0}), std::log(2.));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Intercept 0.000000000; slope 0.693147181; expected slope 0.693147181.
\image(figures/logistic_regression_groups.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Two empirical frequencies determine the intercept and slope of this tiny fit.)
\alttext(Two empirical frequencies determine the intercept and slope of this tiny fit.)

> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  auto z =
      n::tensor({-1000, -1000, 0, 0, 1000, 1000}, {6}, n::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
  const auto y = n::tensor({0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1}, {6}, n::DType::Float64),
             loss = n::sigmoid_cross_entropy(z, y, n::Reduction::None),
             g = n::grad(n::sum(loss), {z})[0];
  const std::vector<double> expected{0, -1, .5, -.5, 1, 0};
  for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i)
    if (!std::isfinite(loss.at({i})) || std::abs(g.at({i}) - expected[i]) > 1e-12)
      return 1;
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("logit", z.to_vector<double>());
  t.add_column("label", y.to_vector<double>());
  t.add_column("NLL", loss.to_vector<double>());
  t.add_column("score", g.to_vector<double>());
  std::print("{}\n", t.to_md(8));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| logit | label | NLL | score |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| -1000 | 0 | 0.00000000 | 0.0 |
| -1000 | 1 | 1000.00000000 | -1.0 |
| 0 | 0 | 0.69314718 | 0.5 |
| 0 | 1 | 0.69314718 | -0.5 |
| 1000 | 0 | 1000.00000000 | 1.0 |
| 1000 | 1 | 0.00000000 | 0.0 |
// result_end

>> Multinomial normalization

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto z = n::tensor({0, std::log(2.), std::log(3.)}, {1, 3}, n::DType::Float64),
             p = n::softmax(z, 1);
  if (std::abs(n::sum(p).item() - 1) > 1e-12)
    return 1;
  std::print("Three-class probabilities: {}.\n", p.to_vector<double>());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Three-class probabilities: [0.16666666666666666, 0.3333333333333333, 0.5].
// result_end


> Conclusion

The fitted grouped frequencies agree with the exact finite optimum, and extreme logits retain
finite losses and correct scores. Probability normalization is necessary but does not establish
calibration or decision utility on new observations.

> References

- [TensorFlow sigmoid cross entropy with logits: stable formulation](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/nn/sigmoid_cross_entropy_with_logits).
